{"about":"76f244935b33bbf83db0551188644d263d71a3214f33e938febbbeaa42dae9a7","body":"The conjecture claims that preferring a well-corroborated theory is a 'logical deduction' about the current state of knowledge, but this mischaracterizes the nature of the preference. Popper explicitly denied that past survival implies future reliability; therefore, deducing that one *should* act on the well-corroborated theory necessarily involves an inductive leap—the assumption that the future will resemble the past. By framing the preference as merely minimizing 'known unknowns,' the conjecture smuggles in a pragmatic induction: it assumes that the mechanism which allowed the theory to survive previously will continue to operate, which is precisely the principle of uniformity of nature that Popper rejected.\n[would settle: A clear demonstration from Popper's own texts showing he endorsed acting on corroborated theories without any implicit assumption that their past success is relevant to their future performance.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}